Mild Immune Thrombocytopenia (ITP)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Platelet count safely maintained between 50-100k, no petechiae or mucosal bleeding

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-030
Reason for Visit: Acute panic attack with somatic symptoms triggered by online pathology results showing low platelets, requiring exclusion of acute bleeding.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute severe anxiety, headache, and fear of spontaneous intracranial hemorrhage in a patient who discovered an abnormal platelet count online. Immediate clinical review was necessary to exclude spontaneous mucosal bleeding, petechiae, or focal neurological deficits indicating a true bleeding emergency prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I saw my blood test results and my platelets are dangerously low. My head is throbbing and I am absolutely convinced I am bleeding into my brain.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports asymptomatic threshold low platelets (count 75k) identified on routine screening. Two hours ago, the patient viewed the result on a patient portal and subsequently suffered an acute panic attack. Symptoms include a severe tension-type headache, tachycardia, and a persistent fear of impending stroke. The patient has been checking their skin obsessively for bruises. Denies epistaxis, gingival bleeding, hematuria, melena, or sudden visual/motor changes.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High health anxiety causing acute distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, hyperventilating slightly, visibly extremely anxious.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam is completely clear; strictly no petechiae, purpura, or spontaneous ecchymoses anywhere on the body. Oropharynx is clear with no hemorrhagic bullae or gingival bleeding. Neurological exam reveals intact cranial nerves, normal bilateral power and sensation, and normal gait. Cardiovascular exam shows sympathetically driven sinus tachycardia. History confirms platelet count safely maintained between 50-100k, no petechiae or mucosal bleeding.
Environmental Observations: Bright room, patient sitting at a computer with medical websites open.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current antiplatelet or anticoagulant medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Health Anxiety and Somatic Headache secondary to incidental Mild Immune Thrombocytopenia (ITP) findings - Status: Stable without hemorrhage
Differentials:

Spontaneous Intracranial Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Completely normal neurological exam, absence of vomiting, and a headache entirely consistent with muscle tension rather than a sudden "thunderclap" presentation.

Severe/Critical Thrombocytopenia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of wet purpura (oral bleeding) or petechial rash, which reliably precede spontaneous major bleeding.

Acute Leukemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of bone pain, hepatosplenomegaly, and no signs of severe anemia or systemic toxicity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe autonomic arousal and a headache driven by catastrophic misinterpretation of a mild thrombocytopenia lab result. Triage correctly mandated physical assessment to definitively exclude spontaneous bleeding or neurological compromise. Comprehensive clinical examination established a complete absence of mucocutaneous bleeding and a normal neurological baseline. This direct bedside reassurance instantly de-escalated the patient's panic, avoiding an unnecessary, highly distressed presentation to the emergency department for a clinically safe, chronic laboratory abnormality, satisfying the criteria for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the safe threshold of the platelet count. Instructed on relaxation techniques to resolve the tension headache.
Positive Behavioral Change: Log out of the pathology portal immediately and take two simple paracetamol tablets with a glass of water. By removing the visual trigger of the lab result and treating the muscle tension, your headache will subside within 30 minutes, proving that it is caused by stress, not bleeding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment next week to discuss the benign nature of mild ITP and repeat blood counts.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop sudden, severe weakness on one side of your body, if you experience a sudden, explosive headache that is the "worst of your life," or if you begin vomiting fresh blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal hematological review; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Mildly low platelets are a common, stable autoimmune quirk. Spontaneous bleeding rarely occurs unless the count drops below 10-20k, far lower than your current level.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you notice hundreds of tiny, pinprick red spots appearing on your ankles, or if your gums begin bleeding profusely without stopping.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is clearing platelets slightly faster than normal, but you still have more than enough to clot blood perfectly well. Your headache is a stress headache from panic.
Actionable Steps: Stop researching platelet counts online tonight. Your physical exam is perfect. See your GP next week to discuss tracking the levels over time.

